<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gian Singh Rarewala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gian Singh Rarewala was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> politician and former official of Patiala State who served as chief minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a> in 1951–1952 and again in 1954–1955. His Akali-supported coalition government of 1952 collapsed, leading to President's rule in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gian-singh-rarewala.jpg" alt="Gian Singh Rarewala" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gian_Singh_Rarewala_ex_CM.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rarewala was born on 16 December 1901 at Rara village, Payal, in Patiala State, a descendant of Ratan Singh Bhangu, author of the Panth Prakash. His sister Jaswant Kaur married Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupinder-singh-of-patiala">Bhupinder Singh of Patiala</a>. He graduated from Mahindra College in 1924, joined Patiala's judicial service and became a High Court judge, Revenue Commissioner and Revenue Minister of the state, and finally its prime minister from 1946 to 1947. His daughter Nirlep Kaur later sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1944, as Deputy Commissioner of Patiala and president of the Singh Sabha, he urged Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadavindra-singh">Yadavindra Singh</a> to make Punjabi the state's official language and within ten months introduced it in schools, administration and the courts. He represented Patiala in the Constituent Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 28 April 1947. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a> was formed on 15 July 1948, he headed its caretaker government and from 13 January 1949 a broad-based ministry. He presented the union's budgets for 1948–49 and 1949–50 in Punjabi, for which over 600 new terms were coined, and promoted Punjabi typewriters and stenography.</p><p>Elected for Payal as an independent in 1951, he became Chief Minister on 22 April 1952 at the head of a United Front ministry, the first non-Congress chief minister of any state in independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His government was dismissed when President's rule was imposed on 5 March 1953.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rarewala became president of the Shiromani Gurdwara Prabandhak Committee in 1955. After the union merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1956 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, won a seat in the Punjab Legislative Assembly in 1957 and served as Irrigation Minister under Pratap Singh Kairon. Re-elected in 1962 and 1967, he became leader of the opposition in 1967. On 14 December 1968 he left Congress for the Shiromani Akali Dal, probably over differences with Morarji Desai, and remained a member until his death on 31 December 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gian Singh Rarewala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its politics were unstable: after the 1952 elections an Akali-led coalition under <b>Gian Singh Rarewala</b> collapsed, and President's rule was imposed in 1953.</blockquote><a href="/state/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a></li></ul></section>
